><text color="#9643FC" creationdate="D:20250708120701+10'00'" flags="print,nozoom,norotate" inreplyto="db547043-6cde-4a72-853b-deb85186d6c5" date="D:20250708120725+10'00'" name="46e2e696-c57d-42ea-a55a-0a5292c8dcbb" icon="Comment" page="7" rect="41.662491,723.867432,65.662491,747.867432" subject="Sticky Note" title="Alexander Gibson"
><contents-richtext
><body xmlns="http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml" xmlns:xfa="http://www.xfa.org/schema/xfa-data/1.0/" xfa:APIVersion="Acrobat:25.1.0" xfa:spec="2.0.2"
><p dir="ltr"
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-14.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Q14c: </span
><span dir="ltr" style="font-size:-12.0pt;text-align:left;color:#000000;font-weight:normal;font-style:normal"
>Women SEM practitioners, both currently and previously,&#xD;working in elite sport are more likely to work with women&#xD;athletes, which may make them vulnerable for lower remuneration&#xD;and leadership recognition. Increasing this possible disadvantage,&#xD;women currently working in elite sport worked fewer&#xD;hours per week and fewer weeks per year. Women SEM practitioners&#xD;also experience significantly more workplace gender&#xD;harassment than men, including infantilisation, work/family&#xD;policing and gender policing. Affirmative action is needed to&#xD;eliminate gender bias in the SEM workplace. Most SEM roles&#xD;are not advertised, nor do they have formal and transparent&#xD;recruitment processes. Elite sporting organisations in Australia&#xD;need to do better to ensure equal access, opportunities for all&#xD;for roles, drive organisational change and reduce gender bias in&#xD;elite sport.</span
></p
></body
></contents-richtext
><popup flags="print,nozoom,norotate" open="no" page="7" rect="595.276001,655.867432,779.276001,747.867432"
/></text
></annots
><f href="10.1136_bjsports-2023-108035.pdf"
/><ids original="89DFC3BC0C710A498D4A775ACEB15C3C" modified="8AA15A58D21DB2110A000B0A10000000"
/></xfdf
